= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Substitution into the <diffusive flux> gives
$$
J(x,t)=-e^{-\lambda t}D(x)\bigl(u'(x)+\beta V'(x)u(x)\bigr).
$$
The <continuity equation> for this <drift-diffusion equation with a potential> therefore reduces, after cancelling the nonzero exponential, to
$$
\boxed{\bigl[D(u'+\beta V'u)\bigr]'=-\lambda u.}
$$
The reflecting <boundary conditions> become $D(u'+\beta V'u)=0$ at both endpoints. Thus each solution of this <eigenvalue problem> supplies a separated solution of the original <diffusion equation>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <product rule> makes the drift term cancel:
$$
u'+\beta V'u=e^{-\beta V}y'.
$$
Consequently the transformed <eigenvalue problem> is
$$
-\bigl(De^{-\beta V}y'\bigr)'=\lambda e^{-\beta V}y,
\qquad
\boxed{p=De^{-\beta V},\quad q=0,\quad w=e^{-\beta V}.}
$$
Both $p$ and the weight $w$ are positive, as required for this <Sturm-Liouville problem>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For sufficiently smooth functions satisfying the same <boundary conditions>, <integration by parts> gives the boundary form
$$
\begin{aligned}
\langle f,\mathcal Lg\rangle-\langle\mathcal Lf,g\rangle
&=\int_{-L}^L\left[-f^*(pg')'+(pf^{*\prime})'g\right]dx\\
&=\left[p\bigl(f^{*\prime}g-f^*g'\bigr)\right]_{-L}^{L}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The stated endpoint relation annihilates this expression. This proves the required <self-adjoint differential operator> identity; for the usual regular <Sturm-Liouville problem>, separated real <boundary conditions> give a self-adjoint realization on its domain.

In this problem the transformed <diffusive flux> is $J=-e^{-\lambda t}py'$. Thus the physical <boundary conditions> are
$$
\boxed{py'(\pm L)=0,}
$$
equivalently $y'(\pm L)=0$ when the endpoint values of $p$ are positive. In particular $pf'=pg'=0$ at the endpoints, which makes the boundary form vanish directly. The <inner product> used here is the ordinary integral; the weight $w$ enters the <generalized eigenvalue problem>, not this boundary-form calculation.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Apply the identity from (c) to the two real <eigenfunctions>. The equations of their <Sturm-Liouville problem> give
$$
0=\int_{-L}^L\left(f_n\mathcal Lf_m-f_m\mathcal Lf_n\right)dx
=(\lambda_m-\lambda_n)\int_{-L}^Lwf_nf_m\,dx.
$$
Since the <eigenvalues> differ,
$$
\boxed{\int_{-L}^Lw(x)f_n(x)f_m(x)\,dx=0.}
$$
This is <orthogonality> in the <weighted inner product>.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For an admissible nonzero real trial function, <integration by parts> and the zero-<flux> <boundary conditions> reduce the <Rayleigh quotient> to
$$
\frac{\int_{-L}^Ly\mathcal Ly\,dx}{\int_{-L}^Lwy^2\,dx}
=\frac{\int_{-L}^Lp(y')^2\,dx}{\int_{-L}^Lwy^2\,dx}\geq0.
$$
A nonzero constant trial function attains zero. Positivity of $p$ also shows that equality requires $y'=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\lambda_0=0,\qquad f_0(x)=C,\qquad c_{\rm eq}(x)=C e^{-\beta V(x)}.}
$$
If the conserved total amount is $M=\int_{-L}^Lc\,dx$, the normalized <stationary solution> is
$$
\boxed{c_{\rm eq}(x)=\frac{M e^{-\beta V(x)}}{\int_{-L}^Le^{-\beta V(s)}\,ds}.}
$$
Its <diffusive flux> vanishes everywhere. The <Boltzmann distribution> fixes the spatial profile, while the <diffusivity> affects the relaxation toward it. Boundedness of $V$ makes the normalization finite on this finite interval.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
