= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the <harmonic extension in a circular sector>, write a separated <harmonic function> as $F(r)H(\theta)$. The <Laplace equation in polar coordinates> yields
$$
\frac{r(rF')'}{F}=-\frac{H''}{H}=\nu^2.
$$
The angular <Dirichlet boundary conditions> select the <Fourier sine basis>
$$
H_n(\theta)=\sin\frac{n\theta}{2\alpha},\qquad \nu_n=\frac{n}{2\alpha},\quad n=1,2,\ldots.
$$
Indeed, the angular equation has no nonzero solution with nonpositive $\nu^2$: multiplying $-H''=\nu^2H$ by $H$ and integrating gives $\int(H')^2=\nu^2\int H^2$. The radial <Cauchy-Euler equation> has solutions $r^{\nu_n}$ and $r^{-\nu_n}$. Boundedness at the vertex excludes the latter, so <superposition> gives
$$
\boxed{\Phi(r,\theta)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty A_n r^{n/(2\alpha)}\sin\frac{n\theta}{2\alpha}.}
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Fourier orthogonality> relation on this angular interval is
$$
\int_0^{2\pi\alpha}\sin\frac{n\theta}{2\alpha}\sin\frac{m\theta}{2\alpha}\,d\theta
=\pi\alpha\,\delta_{nm}.
$$
Projecting the outer <boundary condition> onto each angular <eigenfunction> gives
$$
\boxed{A_n=\frac{R^{-n/(2\alpha)}}{\pi\alpha}\int_0^{2\pi\alpha}\varphi(\theta)\sin\frac{n\theta}{2\alpha}\,d\theta.}
$$
The solution is **unique** in the class of bounded <harmonic functions> with the prescribed boundary trace. For continuous boundary data, the difference of two solutions is zero on the boundary and bounded at the vertex, so the <maximum principle for harmonic functions> makes it zero throughout the sector. Equivalently all its <Fourier sine series> coefficients vanish. The usual convergence interpretation applies if the data are only piecewise smooth.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Reflecting the solution across $\theta=\pi\alpha$ preserves its <Dirichlet boundary conditions> and outer boundary data, so uniqueness makes the solution reflection-symmetric. Since
$$
\sin\frac{n(2\pi\alpha-\theta)}{2\alpha}=(-1)^{n+1}\sin\frac{n\theta}{2\alpha},
$$
only odd angular <Fourier modes> survive. Put $\rho=(r/R)^{1/(2\alpha)}$ and $\psi=\theta/(2\alpha)$. On the symmetry axis $\psi=\pi/2$, comparison of the convergent <power series> gives
$$
A_{2j+1}R^{(2j+1)/(2\alpha)}(-1)^j=\frac1{(2j+1)!}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\Phi(r,\theta)=\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^j\rho^{2j+1}}{(2j+1)!}\sin\bigl((2j+1)\psi\bigr).}
$$
This is the <imaginary part> of $\sin(\rho e^{i\psi})$, so a useful closed form is
$$
\boxed{\Phi(r,\theta)=\cos\bigl(\rho\cos\psi\bigr)\sinh\bigl(\rho\sin\psi\bigr).}
$$
At either straight edge $\sin\psi=0$, while on the symmetry axis the formula is $\sinh\rho$. Every term is <harmonic> in the sector, and the factorial denominator gives convergence up to $r=R$, verifying the full solution.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
