= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <fundamental solution of the Laplace equation> satisfies $\Delta_{\mathbf r}G_0=\delta(\mathbf r-\mathbf r')$ in the sense of <distributions>, without a boundary condition. Choose it rotationally symmetric about the source. Away from the source, with $s=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|$, the radial <Laplace equation> is $(sG_0')'=0$, giving $G_0=A\log s+B$.

Integrating over a small disc around the source and using the <divergence theorem> fixes the normalization:
$$
1=\int_{s<\varepsilon}\Delta G_0\,dS
=\int_{s=\varepsilon}\frac{\partial G_0}{\partial s}\,dC
=\frac A\varepsilon\,2\pi\varepsilon=2\pi A.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{G_0(\mathbf r;\mathbf r')=\frac1{2\pi}\log|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|+B.}
$$
The additive constant is arbitrary; adding other entire <harmonic functions> is also possible if rotational symmetry is not imposed. The positive sign here follows from the convention $\Delta G_0=+\delta$.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Dirichlet Green function> is the solution, with the source point held fixed, of
$$
\boxed{\Delta_{\mathbf r}G(\mathbf r;\mathbf r')=\delta(\mathbf r-\mathbf r')\text{ in }S,\qquad G(\mathbf r;\mathbf r')=0\text{ on }C.}
$$
Its singularity is the logarithmic one found in (a); subtracting that <fundamental solution> leaves a <harmonic function>. An unbounded region also requires a condition at infinity, here boundedness.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<Green's second identity>, with the outward <normal derivative>, is
$$
\int_S(u\Delta v-v\Delta u)\,dS
=\oint_C\left(u\frac{\partial v}{\partial n}-v\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}\right)dC.
$$
Set $u=\Phi$ and $v=G(\cdot;\mathbf r')$, interpreting the logarithmic singularity by a small excised disc or <distribution theory>. The volume integral is $\Phi(\mathbf r')$ because $\Delta\Phi=0$ and $\Delta G=\delta$. On the boundary $G=0$ and $\Phi=f$, leaving
$$
\boxed{\Phi(\mathbf r')=\oint_C f(\mathbf r)\frac{\partial G(\mathbf r;\mathbf r')}{\partial n}\,dC.}
$$
For the exterior problem below, apply the identity first in a large truncated region. For bounded <harmonic functions> the extra circle at infinity contributes zero: $G$ tends to a constant, its radial derivative is $O(r^{-2})$, and a bounded exterior <harmonic function> also has radial derivative $O(r^{-2})$. This is the condition under which the same boundary representation applies there.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $s=|\mathbf r'|>0$ and $\mathbf p=\mathbf r'/s^2$. For $|\mathbf r|=1$, expanding both squared <Euclidean distances> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
s^2|\mathbf r-\mathbf p|^2
&=s^2-2\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf r'+1\\
&=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|^2.
\end{aligned}
$$
Taking nonnegative square roots proves
$$
\boxed{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|=|\mathbf r'|\,|\mathbf r-\mathbf p|.}
$$
This is the distance identity behind <inversion in a circle> and the <method of images> for a circular boundary.

= e
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For a source in the exterior, its inverse point $\mathbf p$ lies strictly inside the excluded disc. The <method of images> construction of the <bounded exterior disk Green function> therefore allows an opposite logarithmic source there without adding a singularity to the domain. The candidate <Dirichlet Green function> is
$$
\boxed{G(\mathbf r;\mathbf r')=\frac1{2\pi}\left(\log|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|-\log|\mathbf r-\mathbf p|-\log|\mathbf r'|\right).}
$$
Its <Laplacian> in the exterior is exactly $\delta(\mathbf r-\mathbf r')$, since the image singularity is outside that domain. The identity in (d) makes $G=0$ on the unit circle. Moreover
$$
\lim_{|\mathbf r|\to\infty}G(\mathbf r;\mathbf r')=-\frac1{2\pi}\log|\mathbf r'|,
$$
so it obeys the required boundedness condition. These checks establish the stated logarithm-of-a-ratio expression with $A=1/(2\pi)$. Bounded exterior <Dirichlet uniqueness for the Poisson equation> follows by <inversion in a circle> and removal of the bounded singularity at the origin.

= f
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The outward normal of the exterior region points **into the excluded disc**, so it is $-\mathbf r$ on the unit circle. Differentiating the <Dirichlet Green function> from (e), and writing $s=|\mathbf r'|$ and $d=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|$, gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial G}{\partial n}
&=\frac1{2\pi}\left[-\frac{1-\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf r'}{d^2}+\frac{1-\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf p}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf p|^2}\right]\\
&=\frac{s^2-1}{2\pi|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|^2}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $|\mathbf r-\mathbf p|^2=d^2/s^2$ was used. The concentrated boundary data have unit integral, so the boundary representation evaluates this exterior <Poisson kernel> at $\mathbf r=(-1,0)$. Thus the bounded solution on either part of the exterior $x$-axis is
$$
\boxed{\Phi(x',0)=\frac{x'^2-1}{2\pi(x'+1)^2}=\frac{x'-1}{2\pi(x'+1)},\qquad |x'|>1.}
$$
It is positive on both intervals, diverges as $x'\to-1^-$ at the boundary source, and tends to $1/(2\pi)$ as $|x'|\to\infty$. Boundedness at infinity selects this solution; otherwise one could add an arbitrary multiple of $\log|\mathbf r'|$ without changing the circle's boundary data.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $f=u+iv$ and $z=x+iy$. For a positively oriented, piecewise smooth simple closed <contour> bounding a region where $u,v$ have continuous first <partial derivatives>, <Green's theorem> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\oint_C f(z)\,dz
&=\oint_C(u\,dx-v\,dy)+i\oint_C(v\,dx+u\,dy)\\
&=\iint_S(-v_x-u_y)\,dx\,dy+i\iint_S(u_x-v_y)\,dx\,dy.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <Cauchy-Riemann equations> are $u_x=v_y$ and $u_y=-v_x$, so both integrands vanish:
$$
\boxed{\oint_C f(z)\,dz=0.}
$$
This proves <Cauchy's integral theorem> under the standard regularity assumptions for <Green's theorem>. More general admissible <contours> follow by subdivision; every enclosed region must be free of singularities.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <simple pole> at $z_0$ means that $f$ is <holomorphic> in a punctured neighbourhood and $(z-z_0)f(z)$ extends holomorphically to a nonzero value at $z_0$. Equivalently its <Laurent series> begins with $a_{-1}/(z-z_0)$ and has no higher negative powers. The <residue> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Res}(f,z_0)=\lim_{z\to z_0}(z-z_0)f(z).}
$$
In particular, if $f=g/h$ with $g(z_0)\ne0$, $h(z_0)=0$ and $h'(z_0)\ne0$, this becomes $\operatorname{Res}(f,z_0)=g(z_0)/h'(z_0)$. The associated <residue theorem> is
$$
\boxed{\oint_C f(z)\,dz=2\pi i\sum_{z_j\text{ inside }C}\operatorname{Res}(f,z_j)}
$$
for a positively oriented simple <contour> with no poles on it.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The exponential numerator is entire and never zero. The denominator vanishes precisely when $e^{2z}=-1$, giving
$$
z_n=i\pi\left(n+\tfrac12\right),\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.
$$
At these points $\sinh z_n=i(-1)^n\ne0$, so every zero of the denominator is simple and hence every singularity is a <simple pole>. Using the <residue> formula in (b),
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Res}(f,z_n)=\frac{e^{-ikz_n}}{\sinh z_n}=\frac{(-1)^n}{i}\exp\left[k\pi\left(n+\tfrac12\right)\right].}
$$

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use the counterclockwise rectangular <contour> with vertices $-R,R,R+i\pi,-R+i\pi$. It encloses just the <simple pole> $i\pi/2$, whose <residue> is $e^{k\pi/2}/i$. Let $I_R=\int_{-R}^R e^{-ikx}/\cosh x\,dx$. The identity
$$
f(x+i\pi)=-e^{k\pi}f(x)
$$
makes the top edge, traversed right to left, contribute $e^{k\pi}I_R$. On either vertical edge,
$$
|\cosh(\pm R+iy)|^2=\sinh^2R+\cos^2y,\qquad |e^{-ik(\pm R+iy)}|=e^{ky}\leq e^{|k|\pi}.
$$
Thus the sum of the vertical integrals tends to zero, with absolute value at most $2\pi e^{|k|\pi}/\sinh R$. The <residue theorem> and $R\to\infty$ now give
$$
(1+e^{k\pi})I=2\pi e^{k\pi/2},\qquad
\boxed{I=\pi\operatorname{sech}\frac{k\pi}{2}.}
$$
The argument works for every fixed real $k$, including zero and negative values, and gives the <Fourier transform of the hyperbolic secant> with the convention $\widetilde f(k)=\int e^{-ikx}f(x)\,dx$.

= 5
