= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <Fourier transform> convention
$$
\widetilde f(\omega)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-i\omega t}f(t)\,dt,\qquad
f(t)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{i\omega t}\widetilde f(\omega)\,d\omega.
$$
The <convolution> is $(f*g)(t)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(\tau)g(t-\tau)\,d\tau$. For integrable $f,g$, <Fubini's theorem> and the substitution $s=t-\tau$ give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\widetilde{f*g}(\omega)
&=\int_{\mathbb R}\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-i\omega t}f(\tau)g(t-\tau)\,d\tau\,dt\\
&=\left(\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-i\omega\tau}f(\tau)\,d\tau\right)\left(\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-i\omega s}g(s)\,ds\right).
\end{aligned}
$$
Hence the <convolution theorem> is
$$
\boxed{\widetilde{f*g}(\omega)=\widetilde f(\omega)\widetilde g(\omega).}
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take the causal solution, extending the input and output by zero before the device starts at $t=0$. The two zero <initial conditions> ensure that this extension adds no impulsive terms to the first two <distributional derivatives>. The <Fourier transform of a derivative> is multiplication by $i\omega$, so
$$
\left[(p+i\omega)^2+q^2\right]\widetilde\phi(\omega)=\widetilde I(\omega).
$$
Factoring the quadratic and taking <partial fraction decomposition> yields
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\phi=\widetilde R\widetilde I,\qquad
\widetilde R(\omega)=\frac1{2iq}\left(\frac1{p+i\omega-iq}-\frac1{p+i\omega+iq}\right).}
$$
The inverse <Fourier transform> follows directly by integrating decaying exponentials:
$$
\boxed{R(t)=H(t)\frac{e^{-pt}\sin(qt)}q,}
$$
where $H$ is the <Heaviside step function>. This <damped-oscillator impulse response> has $R(0^+)=0$ and $R'(0^+)=1$, as required for a unit impulse.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <convolution theorem> gives the causal response
$$
\phi(t)=\frac{I_0}q\int_0^{\min(t,1)}e^{-p(t-\tau)}\sin\bigl(q(t-\tau)\bigr)\,d\tau\quad(t>0).
$$
Define the integrated <impulse response>, for $s\geq0$, by
$$
F(s)=\frac{1-e^{-ps}\cos(qs)-(p/q)e^{-ps}\sin(qs)}{p^2+q^2}.
$$
Direct differentiation gives $F'(s)=e^{-ps}\sin(qs)/q$ and $F(0)=F'(0)=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\phi(t)=\begin{cases}
0,&t\leq0,\\
I_0F(t),&0<t\leq1,\\
I_0\bigl[F(t)-F(t-1)\bigr],&t>1.
\end{cases}}
$$
In particular, after switch-off the answer is
$$
\boxed{\phi(t)=\frac{I_0}{p^2+q^2}\left\{
e^{-p(t-1)}\left[\cos q(t-1)+\frac pq\sin q(t-1)\right]
-e^{-pt}\left[\cos qt+\frac pq\sin qt\right]\right\},\quad t>1.}
$$
The response and its first <derivative> are continuous at both switching times, and the post-pulse motion is a freely decaying <damped harmonic oscillator>. The expression described as valid for $t<1$ in the paper is understood for $0<t<1$; before the pulse the causal output is zero.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use the same <Fourier transform> convention, now in the sense of <tempered distributions>, since the homogeneous oscillations are not integrable. Transforming gives
$$
\bigl[q^2-(\omega+r)^2\bigr]\widetilde\phi(\omega)=0.
$$
Since $q>0$, the two roots are distinct. A <distribution> annihilated by this polynomial is a linear combination of <Dirac delta distributions> at its roots:
$$
\widetilde\phi(\omega)=2\pi A\delta(\omega+r-q)+2\pi B\delta(\omega+r+q).
$$
<Fourier inversion> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\phi(t)=A e^{i(q-r)t}+B e^{-i(q+r)t}
=e^{-irt}\bigl(C\cos qt+D\sin qt\bigr).}
$$
The constants may be complex. Differentiating the factor $e^{-irt}$ also verifies that the original <ordinary differential equation> reduces to $\ddot\psi+q^2\psi=0$ for $\phi=e^{-irt}\psi$.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a Cartesian coordinate change $x'_i=R_{ij}x_j$ with $R$ an <orthogonal matrix>, a rank-$n$ <tensor> obeys
$$
\boxed{T'_{i_1\cdots i_n}=R_{i_1j_1}\cdots R_{i_nj_n}T_{j_1\cdots j_n},}
$$
with repeated indices summed according to <Einstein notation>. A <tensor> is **isotropic** if $T'_{i_1\cdots i_n}=T_{i_1\cdots i_n}$ for every proper <rotation matrix> $R$. Under proper rotations the <Levi-Civita symbol> is an <isotropic third-rank tensor>; if reflections are included, it is a <pseudotensor>. The convention using proper rotations is the one relevant to the rank-three classification here.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Under a proper <rotation matrix> $R$, the product of coordinates transforms as a rank-$n$ <tensor>. The unit ball is unchanged by the substitution $\mathbf y=R\mathbf x$, whose <Jacobian determinant> is one. Hence
$$
\begin{aligned}
R_{i_1j_1}\cdots R_{i_nj_n}S_{j_1\cdots j_n}
&=\int_B(R\mathbf x)_{i_1}\cdots(R\mathbf x)_{i_n}\,dV_{\mathbf x}\\
&=\int_B y_{i_1}\cdots y_{i_n}\,dV_{\mathbf y}=S_{i_1\cdots i_n}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus $S$ is an <isotropic tensor>. An <isotropic second-rank tensor> has the form $S_{ij}=A\delta_{ij}$. Taking the <trace> and using <spherical coordinates>,
$$
3A=\int_B|\mathbf x|^2\,dV=4\pi\int_0^1r^4\,dr=\frac{4\pi}5.
$$
Therefore the <isotropic second moment of a ball> is
$$
\boxed{S_{ij}=\frac{4\pi}{15}\delta_{ij}.}
$$
An <isotropic third-rank tensor> has the form $B\epsilon_{ijk}$, but $S_{ijk}$ is symmetric in every pair of indices, forcing $B=0$. Alternatively, $\mathbf x\mapsto-\mathbf x$ makes its integrand odd on the ball. Thus
$$
\boxed{S_{ijk}=0.}
$$

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Choose an <orthonormal basis> with $\mathbf n=\mathbf e_3$. A half-turn about $\mathbf n$ kills all mixed axial-transverse components. Commutation with a quarter-turn makes the transverse block a scalar <identity matrix> plus an <antisymmetric matrix>. The resulting <oriented-axis invariant Cartesian tensor>, in coordinate-free form, is
$$
\boxed{\sigma_{ij}=a(\delta_{ij}-n_in_j)+d\,n_in_j+h\epsilon_{ikj}n_k,}
$$
with real constants $a,d,h$. The <Kronecker delta>, the <outer product> $n_in_j$, and the displayed <Levi-Civita symbol> contraction are invariant under every proper rotation about $\mathbf n$, so this form is also sufficient. In the chosen basis,
$$
\sigma=\begin{pmatrix}a&-h&0\\h&a&0\\0&0&d\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The antisymmetric part must be retained unless further symmetry is imposed.

Decompose the <electric field> as $\mathbf E=\mathbf E_\perp+\mathbf E_\parallel$, where $\mathbf E_\parallel=(\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf E)\mathbf n$. The <electric current density> is
$$
\mathbf J=a\mathbf E_\perp+d\mathbf E_\parallel+h\mathbf n\times\mathbf E_\perp.
$$
For a nonzero real <electric field>, the <alignment of current and field in an axisymmetric conductor> gives the complete classification of <collinearity>:

* If $\mathbf E_\perp=0$, then $\boxed{\mathbf J=d\mathbf E}$, with no additional symmetry required.
* If $\mathbf E_\parallel=0$, then $\boxed{\mathbf J\parallel\mathbf E\ \Longleftrightarrow\ h=0}$. Within this family $h=0$ is exactly the condition that the <conductivity tensor> is symmetric; then $\mathbf J=a\mathbf E$. An additional half-turn symmetry about a transverse axis is one way to force $h=0$.
* If both components are nonzero, then $\boxed{\mathbf J\parallel\mathbf E\ \Longleftrightarrow\ h=0\text{ and }a=d}$. These conditions give the <isotropic second-rank tensor> $\sigma=aI$, and then $\mathbf J=a\mathbf E$ for every direction.

For completeness, if $\mathbf E_\perp\ne0$ and $\mathbf J=\lambda\mathbf E$, taking the <dot product> with $\mathbf n\times\mathbf E_\perp$ forces $h=0$. The transverse equation then gives $\lambda=a$, while a nonzero axial component gives $\lambda=d$. This proves there are no missing directions. Zero current is included as the zero scalar multiple; if “parallel” requires a direction for $\mathbf J$, exclude cases where that scalar is zero. For a strictly anisotropic crystal the last possibility is absent.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

An axial <electric field> produces $\mathbf J=d\mathbf E$, so the observation sets $d=0$ and imposes no restriction on the transverse or antisymmetric parts. Thus
$$
\boxed{\sigma_{ij}=a(\delta_{ij}-n_in_j)+h\epsilon_{ikj}n_k.}
$$
A symmetric <conductivity tensor> would further require $h=0$, but axial rotational invariance alone does not require this.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the bead <angles> $\theta_1,\theta_2,\theta_3$ as <generalized coordinates>. The straight-line spring length between two beads follows from the <law of cosines>:
$$
\ell_{ij}=\sqrt{2-2\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j)}.
$$
The hoop is horizontal, so the <gravitational potential energy> is constant and can be omitted. The <kinetic energy> is $(m/2)\sum_i\dot\theta_i^2$, giving the <Lagrangian>
$$
\boxed{L=\frac m2\sum_{i=1}^3\dot\theta_i^2-\frac k2\sum_{i<j}\left[\sqrt{2-2\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j)}-\sqrt3\right]^2.}
$$
At $\theta_i^0=\Theta+2\pi(i-1)/3$, every spring has its natural length, so the <potential energy> is zero and its first <derivatives> vanish. This is an <equilibrium> for every constant $\Theta$. It is stable against relative displacement because each spring energy is nonnegative; the overall rotation is a neutral direction.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $\theta_i=\theta_i^0+\eta_i$. Differentiating the spring length gives
$$
\delta\ell_{ij}=\frac{\sin(\theta_i^0-\theta_j^0)}{\sqrt3}(\eta_i-\eta_j),\qquad
(\delta\ell_{ij})^2=\frac14(\eta_i-\eta_j)^2.
$$
The springs are unextended at <equilibrium>, so the quadratic <potential energy> contains only the squares of these first-order extensions:
$$
U_2=\frac k8\left[(\eta_1-\eta_2)^2+(\eta_2-\eta_3)^2+(\eta_3-\eta_1)^2\right].
$$
The linearized <Euler-Lagrange equations> are therefore
$$
m\ddot{\boldsymbol\eta}+\frac k4
\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&-1\\-1&2&-1\\-1&-1&2\end{pmatrix}\boldsymbol\eta=0.
$$
The <stiffness matrix> is $(k/4)(3I-\mathbf 1\mathbf 1^T)$. Its <eigenvector> $\mathbf 1=(1,1,1)^T$ has zero <eigenvalue>, and every vector whose components sum to zero has <eigenvalue> $3k/4$. One convenient orthonormal set of <normal modes> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\mathbf v_0&=\frac1{\sqrt3}(1,1,1)^T,&\omega_0&=0,\\
\mathbf v_1&=\frac1{\sqrt2}(1,-1,0)^T,&\omega_1&=\sqrt{\frac{3k}{4m}},\\
\mathbf v_2&=\frac1{\sqrt6}(1,1,-2)^T,&\omega_2&=\sqrt{\frac{3k}{4m}}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
Any orthonormal basis of the sum-zero plane is equally valid for the two degenerate <normal modes>. The general small motion is
$$
\boldsymbol\eta(t)=(A_0+B_0t)\mathbf v_0+\sum_{j=1}^2\left[A_j\cos(\omega_1t)+B_j\sin(\omega_1t)\right]\mathbf v_j.
$$
The linear drift in the <zero mode> describes a slow uniform rotation. These are <angular frequencies>; the ordinary <frequencies> of the two vibrational modes are $\omega_1/(2\pi)$.

\Image[paper-2-q7-normal-modes.png]
{title=Tangential displacement patterns of the three bead normal modes}
{description=Arrows show the signs and relative sizes of the angular displacements in one choice of orthonormal <normal modes>. The equal-arrow pattern is a rigid rotation, while the two sum-zero patterns have equal nonzero <angular frequency>.}

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A common shift of all three <angles> leaves every spring length unchanged. This continuous rotational <symmetry> gives the <zero mode>: there is no restoring force against rigid rotation.

The equilateral <equilibrium> also has the <dihedral group> symmetry $D_3$. Its action on the three angular displacements preserves the uniform one-dimensional subspace and the two-dimensional sum-zero subspace. The latter is an <irreducible representation> over the <real numbers>; a reflection may reverse all angular signs as well as exchange bead labels, which does not change these invariant subspaces. The symmetric <stiffness matrix> must act as a scalar on the sum-zero subspace, explaining the equal vibrational <angular frequencies>. Directly, each sum-zero vector satisfies $(3I-\mathbf1\mathbf1^T)\mathbf v=3\mathbf v$. This is an example of <degenerate normal modes from permutation symmetry>, and of <normal modes of three equal masses with a symmetric gap potential>.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <group order> is its <cardinality>, $\boxed{|G|=\#\{g:g\in G\}}$. The <order of a group element> $g$ is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}(g)=\min\{n\geq1:g^n=e\},}
$$
where $e$ is the <identity element>. In a <finite group> such an $n$ always exists, since two nonnegative powers of $g$ must coincide.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Lagrange's theorem> states that for a <subgroup> $H$ of a <finite group> $G$,
$$
\boxed{|G|=[G:H]\,|H|,\qquad |H|\mid |G|.}
$$
The left <cosets> of $H$ partition $G$, and multiplication by a coset representative is a <bijection> from $H$ onto that coset, explaining the formula. If $|G|=p$ is <prime>, then $|H|$ is either $1$ or $p$, so $H=\{e\}$ or $H=G$. Thus the correct conclusion is **no proper nontrivial subgroup**. The <trivial subgroup> $\{e\}$ is proper for every group of prime order, so the wording in the question needs this qualification.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $d=\operatorname{ord}(g)$. The powers
$$
\langle g\rangle=\{e,g,\ldots,g^{d-1}\}
$$
are distinct: equality of two with exponents between $0$ and $d-1$ would give a smaller positive power equal to $e$. Reducing exponents modulo $d$ proves closure and supplies inverses, so this is a <cyclic subgroup> of <group order> $d$. <Lagrange's theorem> now gives
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}(g)=|\langle g\rangle|\mid |G|.}
$$

= d
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Choose $g\ne e$ in a group of prime <group order> $p$. Its <order of a group element> divides $p$ by (c) and is not $1$, hence equals $p$. Consequently its <cyclic subgroup> already contains all elements of $G$:
$$
\boxed{G=\langle g\rangle\cong C_p.}
$$
Thus every nonidentity element is a <group generator>.

= e
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

By (d), every group of <group order> three is a <cyclic group>. Writing its elements as $e,a,a^2$, every product is determined by $a^3=e$; the <multiplication table> is
$$
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
\cdot&e&a&a^2\\\hline
e&e&a&a^2\\
a&a&a^2&e\\
a^2&a^2&e&a
\end{array}
$$
The map $a^j\mapsto j\pmod3$ is a bijective <group homomorphism> to addition modulo three. Therefore
$$
\boxed{G\cong\mathbb Z_3,}
$$
so there is exactly one such <group> **up to isomorphism**.

= f
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Closure of the componentwise operation follows from closure in each factor. For three pairs, <associativity> holds coordinatewise:
$$
\bigl((g_1,g_2)(h_1,h_2)\bigr)(k_1,k_2)
=\bigl((g_1h_1)k_1,(g_2h_2)k_2\bigr)
=\bigl(g_1(h_1k_1),g_2(h_2k_2)\bigr).
$$
The <identity element> is $(e_1,e_2)$ and the <inverse element> of $(g_1,g_2)$ is $(g_1^{-1},g_2^{-1})$. These verify all <group axioms> for the <direct product of groups>. Counting independent choices gives
$$
\boxed{|G_1\times G_2|=|G_1|\,|G_2|.}
$$
The subset $N=G_1\times\{e_2\}$ contains the identity and is closed under products and inverses, so it is a <subgroup>. For any $x=(a,b)$ in the product and $(g,e_2)\in N$,
$$
x(g,e_2)x^{-1}=(aga^{-1},be_2b^{-1})=(aga^{-1},e_2)\in N.
$$
Conjugation by $x^{-1}$ gives the reverse inclusion, and hence
$$
\boxed{xNx^{-1}=N,\qquad N\mathrel{\trianglelefteq}G_1\times G_2.}
$$
This is the definition of a <normal subgroup>.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Both <subgroups> contain the <identity element>, so $H\cap K$ is nonempty. If $x,y\in H\cap K$, then $xy\in H$ and $xy\in K$, so $xy\in H\cap K$. Similarly $x^{-1}$ belongs to both subgroups and hence to their intersection. <Associativity> is inherited from $G$. Thus
$$
\boxed{H\cap K\leq G.}
$$
This verifies the <group axioms>; finiteness is unnecessary.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In additive notation every element $(a,b)\in\mathbb Z_2\times\mathbb Z_4$ obeys $4(a,b)=(0,0)$. Its <order of a group element> therefore divides four, whereas $1\in\mathbb Z_8$ has order eight. A <group isomorphism> preserves element orders, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb Z_2\times\mathbb Z_4\not\cong\mathbb Z_8.}
$$
For the second product, define the <group homomorphism>
$$
\Psi:\mathbb Z_6\longrightarrow\mathbb Z_2\times\mathbb Z_3,\qquad
[t]_6\longmapsto([t]_2,[t]_3).
$$
It is well-defined because multiples of six are divisible by both two and three. Its <kernel> contains only zero: simultaneous divisibility by two and three implies divisibility by six. Thus $\Psi$ is injective, and equal finite <group orders> make it surjective. Explicitly the six images are
$$
(0,0),(1,1),(0,2),(1,0),(0,1),(1,2).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb Z_2\times\mathbb Z_3\cong\mathbb Z_6.}
$$
For general positive integers $n,m$, the <order of a direct-product element> is the <least common multiple> of its component orders. Hence every element order divides $\operatorname{lcm}(n,m)=nm/\gcd(n,m)$, while $(1,1)$ attains this order. It generates the whole product precisely when $n,m$ are <coprime>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb Z_n\times\mathbb Z_m\cong\mathbb Z_{nm}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\gcd(n,m)=1.}
$$
This is the <cyclicity of a product of two finite cyclic groups>, with the explicit isomorphism furnished by the <Chinese remainder theorem>.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <center of a group> contains $e$. If $z,w$ lie in the center, then for every $g\in G$,
$$
g(zw)g^{-1}=(gzg^{-1})(gwg^{-1})=zw,
\qquad
gz^{-1}g^{-1}=(gzg^{-1})^{-1}=z^{-1}.
$$
It is therefore closed under the <group operation> and <inverse elements>, and inherits <associativity>, proving it is a <subgroup>. Each of its elements commutes with every element of $G$, in particular with every other central element. Hence
$$
\boxed{Z(G)\leq G\text{ and }zw=wz\text{ for all }z,w\in Z(G).}
$$
Thus the center is an <abelian subgroup>.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <group homomorphism> preserves multiplication:
$$
\boxed{\Phi(g_1g_2)=\Phi(g_1)\Phi(g_2)\quad\text{for every }g_1,g_2\in G.}
$$
It automatically sends the <identity element> to the identity and sends <inverse elements> to inverses. A <group isomorphism> is a **bijective group homomorphism**. For finite groups this requires equal <group orders>, and, once those orders agree, either injectivity or surjectivity of the <group homomorphism> is sufficient. Equal orders alone do not suffice.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Here $a^2=-I$, $a^3=-a$ and $a^4=I$, so $K$ is a <cyclic group of order four>. Its <multiplication table> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
\cdot&I&-I&a&-a\\\hline
I&I&-I&a&-a\\
-I&-I&I&-a&a\\
a&a&-a&-I&I\\
-a&-a&a&I&-I
\end{array}}
$$
Write the specified image of $a$ as $A$. Direct matrix multiplication gives
$$
A^2=-I_2,\qquad A^3=-A,\qquad A^4=I_2.
$$
A <group representation> is a <group homomorphism> to a <general linear group>, so the images of all powers of $a$ are forced:
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\Phi(I)&=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\\
\Phi(-I)&=\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\\
\Phi(-a)&=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\-i&0\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
All four images are invertible. Defining $\Phi(a^j)=A^j$ is well-defined modulo four because $A^4=I_2$, and $A^{j+\ell}=A^jA^\ell$ verifies every product in the table. These observations prove both existence and uniqueness of this <representation> with the prescribed image.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Distinguish the <quaternion group> generators $\mathbf i,\mathbf j,\mathbf k$ from the complex scalar $i$. Denote their proposed image matrices by
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B=\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\i&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
C=\begin{pmatrix}i&0\\0&-i\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Direct multiplication gives
$$
A^2=B^2=C^2=-I_2,\qquad
AB=C,\quad BC=A,\quad CA=B,\qquad
BA=-C,\quad CB=-A,\quad AC=-B.
$$
For example $AB=\operatorname{diag}(i,-i)=C$ and $ABC=C^2=-I_2$. Sending $I$ to $I_2$ and each negative quaternion element to the negative of its positive image now reproduces every quaternion product. The matrices all have <determinant> one and are invertible, so this is a <group representation> in $GL(2,\mathbb C)$.

The eight matrices $\pm I_2,\pm A,\pm B,\pm C$ are distinct. Therefore the <group homomorphism> is injective, and the representation is **faithful**; equivalently its <kernel> is $\{I\}$.

The <character of a representation> is the <trace> of its representing matrix. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\chi(I)=2,\qquad\chi(-I)=-2,\qquad
\chi(\pm\mathbf i)=\chi(\pm\mathbf j)=\chi(\pm\mathbf k)=0.}
$$
These are constant on the five <conjugacy classes> $\{I\}$, $\{-I\}$, $\{\pm\mathbf i\}$, $\{\pm\mathbf j\}$, $\{\pm\mathbf k\}$.

The complete list of <subgroups> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{subgroup}&\text{order}\\\hline
\{I\}&1\\
\{I,-I\}&2\\
\langle\mathbf i\rangle=\{I,-I,\mathbf i,-\mathbf i\}&4\\
\langle\mathbf j\rangle=\{I,-I,\mathbf j,-\mathbf j\}&4\\
\langle\mathbf k\rangle=\{I,-I,\mathbf k,-\mathbf k\}&4\\
Q&8
\end{array}}
$$
To see completeness, <Lagrange's theorem> allows only orders $1,2,4,8$. The only element of order two is $-I$, which fixes the order-two subgroup. Every noncentral element has order four and generates the corresponding listed <cyclic subgroup>. Any order-four subgroup contains such an element, because there are only two central elements; it must equal the <cyclic subgroup> generated by that element. This accounts for all possibilities.
